NAME
    swap - disk-backed "slow RAM": status and maintenance

SYNOPSIS
    swap
    swap keys
    swap clear

DESCRIPTION
    OpenComputers has no transparent virtual memory, so TOS cannot magically
    extend computer.totalMemory(). swap is an EXPLICIT spill-to-disk layer for
    cold data, backed by /var/swap. Programs use it to offload large data they
    don't need resident and page it back on demand.

    The 'swap' command reports status; the swap API is used from code:
      swap.store(key, value) / swap.fetch(key) / swap.free(key)
      swap.table{ hot = N }   a table whose entries live on disk with an N-entry
                              in-RAM LRU cache; reads/writes feel like a normal
                              table (honors # and pairs()).

    Swap is VOLATILE by design: /var/swap is wiped on every boot (like RAM, and
    to clear any crash debris). It is size-capped by swapMaxKB in /etc/tos.cfg
    (default 4 MB), auto-clamped so it can never fill the disk; over-budget
    writes fail loudly rather than corrupt anything.

    Sandboxed programs receive only the self-namespacing swap.table API via the
    "swap" capability — one program cannot read another's swap data.

    COMPRESSION: when a data card is present, spilled data is deflated before it
    hits disk (and inflated on fetch), so more logical data fits under the cap.
    This is automatic and detection-gated — on a card-less box swap stores raw
    bytes exactly as before. The status line shows "compressed" when active.
    See 'man compress' for the file-level commands using the same mechanism.

SUBCOMMANDS
    (none)   Show used/cap/percent and entry count.
    keys     List the store keys currently held.
    clear    Wipe all swap now (admin).

EXAMPLES
    swap
    swap clear

SEE ALSO
    man memory, man compress, MANUAL.md (Section 9.3)
